==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|aspic}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|aspis}}, and possibly influenced by French basilic ‎(“basilisk”). The culinary sense may come from association with the snake due to the traditional colours and cold temperature of aspic, in addition to the fact that the gelatine was often moulded in a shape similar to a coiled snake.

===Noun===
[[Image:Aspic-with-eggs.jpg|thumb|aspic with eggs]]
{{en-noun}}

# A dish in which ingredients are set into a [[gelatine]], [[jelly]]-like substance made from a [[meat stock]] or [[consommé]].
# {{label|en|obsolete|poetic}} An [[asp]], a small venomous snake of Egypt.
#* '''1623''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, {{w|Antony and Cleopatra}}:
#*: This is an '''aspic's''' trail: and these fig-leaves / Have slime upon them, such as the '''aspic''' leaves.
#* '''1833''', [[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Lord Alfred Tennyson]], {{w|A Dream of Fair Women}}:
#*: (With that she tore her robe apart, and half / The polished argent of her breast to sight / Laid bare. Thereto she pointed with a laugh, / Showing the '''aspic's''' bite. )
# A piece of [[ordnance]] carrying a 12-pound [[shot]].

